From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 17:45:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: drain audio instead of flushing it

The audio broke for a moment every so often, most noticeably while a game was
loading. Kodi flushes the whole audio stream once the sink delay passes 300ms,
and everything already queued goes with it - that is what is heard.

The delay creeps past the threshold whenever a client stalls and then catches
up, which a disc load does reliably, and it was only ever a few milliseconds
over when it fired:

    RetroPlayer[AUDIO]: Audio delay (306.12 ms) is too high - flushing
    RetroPlayer[AUDIO]: Audio delay (306.03 ms) is too high - flushing

Skipping the packet instead gives the sink one packet's worth of time to drain
and brings the delay back under within a few frames, inaudibly. Flushing is
kept for a delay far enough out that something is genuinely wrong.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerAudio.cpp	2026-09-07 17:44:01.670932072 +1200
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/streams/RetroPlayerAudio.cpp	2026-09-07 17:44:01.689962679 +1200
@@ -25,6 +25,10 @@
 
 const double MAX_DELAY = 0.3; // seconds
 
+// Only worth throwing the buffer away past this. Between MAX_DELAY and here
+// the excess is worked off a packet at a time instead - see AddStreamData().
+const double FLUSH_DELAY = 1.0; // seconds
+
 // How long to stay quiet between log lines.
 const std::chrono::seconds DROP_LOG_INTERVAL{10};
 
@@ -124,6 +128,22 @@
 
       if (delaySecs > MAX_DELAY)
       {
+        // Work the excess off a packet at a time rather than flushing.
+        //
+        // Flushing drops everything already queued, which is heard as a break
+        // in the sound. The delay creeps past MAX_DELAY on its own whenever a
+        // client stalls and then catches up - a disc load is the usual cause -
+        // and it was only ever a few milliseconds over:
+        //
+        //   RetroPlayer[AUDIO]: Audio delay (306.12 ms) is too high - flushing
+        //
+        // Skipping this packet gives the sink one packet's worth of time to
+        // drain, which brings it back under in a handful of frames and is not
+        // audible. A flush is kept for a delay far enough out that something
+        // is genuinely wrong.
+        if (delaySecs <= FLUSH_DELAY)
+          return;
+
         m_pAudioStream->Flush();
         CLog::Log(LOGDEBUG, "RetroPlayer[AUDIO]: Audio delay ({:0.2f} ms) is too high - flushing",
                   delaySecs * 1000);
